    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d398">

                <pb facs="0420" n="416" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg416"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/14501: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-12-14T17:00:00+09:00">December 14, 1938—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 15—3:25 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>783. Our 757, November 30, 3 p.m., last sentence.</p>
                <list>
                    <label>1.</label>
                    <item>Absence of recent telegrams from China and the complete silence maintained
                        by the Japanese press on this subject make it difficult for <pb facs="0421"
                            n="417" xml:id="pg417"/> us to appraise recent events affecting the
                        Japanese plans to set up a new central Chinese Government. However,
                        yesterday the Vice Minister for Foreign Affairs<anchor n="62"
                            xml:id="d398-f1"/> informed us confidentially and off the record that
                        the Foreign Office considers Wu Pei Fu’s<anchor n="63" xml:id="d398-f2"/>
                        refusal to head the proposed new government to be a blessing in disguise as
                        the Foreign Office favors a federal form of government for China and not one
                        which would have assimilated the existing regimes at Peiping and Nanking.
                        Sawada said that all negotiations with Wu have ceased, that Wu is not
                        popular in Central and South China and that there is available no Chinese
                        who would receive as Chief of Government general support in all Japanese
                        occupied parts of China. He further said that the Foreign Office envisages
                        the establishment of local governments additional to those already set up
                        and the linking together of these governments by a “Central Committee” the
                        members of which would elect a chairman from among themselves.</item>
                    <label>2.</label>
                    <item>In view of the foregoing the decision to postpone indefinitely the
                        proposed announcement (which was to have been made by the Prime Minister on
                        December 11) with regard to the policy decided upon by the conference held
                        on November 30 in the presence of the Emperor may now be put down with a
                        reasonable degree of certainty to drastic changes in the Japanese plans
                        occasioned by Wu Pei Fu’s decision.</item>
                </list>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
